1. Introduction
[Marks: 5 (Knowledge & Information) – Convey the motivation and background knowledge/theory necessary to understand the remainder of the report.]
[TOP MARKS TIP: Explanations criterion (5 marks) applies throughout: for every topic, always explain WHAT was done, WHY, and HOW.]
Hospital environments require the rapid and efficient delivery of medicines and other necessary items to wards and patients. Conventional manual delivery methods are time-consuming and labour-intensive and have the disadvantage of potential human error and delays in critical situations. This project aims to develop a prototype electric vehicle to simulate an autonomous hospital delivery robot, capable of transporting such payloads more efficiently and accurately than conventional methods.

The prototype utilises two BBC Micro:bits [1], one of which interfaces with a Kitronik compact robotics board [2] to control four DC motors using pulse width modulation. The second micro:bit serves as a handheld wireless transmitter, allowing manual override and mode switching via the integrated radio communication protocol. For autonomous navigation, a Kitronik 5629 line follower [3] is paired with an RCWL-1601 ultrasonic sensor [4], enabling the vehicle to follow a predefined route whilst detecting obstacles. The prototype is constructed using a Waveshare Robot Chassis-P [5], a four-wheeled aluminium alloy platform driven by four DC motors [6]. The payload is secured within an enclosed compartment mounted to the upper chassis of the vehicle, designed to safely contain and transport medical supplies during delivery operations.



1.1 Project Background and Motivation
[Explain why this EV project matters. What problem does it solve? What is the broader context of electric vehicles? Include relevant background theory.]

Modern hospital environments require continuous routine and urgent delivery of essential items such as medicine, laboratory samples, meals, and clean linen. These logistical tasks are currently performed manually by medical staff, placing a significant burden on an already overstretched workforce. Figures show, in England alone NHS waiting lists reached approximately 7.4 million patients by August 2025 [8], this extensive pressure on healthcare requires modern solutions to be explored to free healthcare workers from laborious dutys that could instead become automated. Furthermore, any opportunity to rotate staff time from transport duties to direct patient care, represents a meaningful improvement in overall operational efficiency.
The healthcare sector has identified the potential of robotic automation to address time consuming tasks. As of 2024, the global medical robotic systems market was valued at approximately $25.6 billion, this figure is projected to grow at an annual growth rate of 16.6% through to 2030 [7]. A notable real-world example is the “TUG” autonomous mobile robot, manufactured by Aethon and deployed at the UCSF Medical Centre in San Francisco, this autonomous hospital robot delivers supplies and collects clinical waste, reducing the logistical task load for hospital workers and increasing patient-facing task capability [9].
This project seeks to explore the potential of increased efficiency using previous principles through a scaled-down prototype that simulates an autonomous hospital delivery robot with the aforementioned electric motors, line-following navigation, ultrasonic sensor and wireless communication.

1.2 Social, Legal, and Environmental Evaluation
[Marks: 5 (Critical Thinking) – Critically evaluate the EV concept weighing social, legal, and environmental aspects. Security aspects can also be considered.]
[TOP MARKS TIP: Go beyond description – critically EVALUATE. Weigh pros against cons, reference legislation or standards, and explain implications for your design decisions.]
The introduction of autonomous delivery robots in hospital settings offers clear social benefits. By automating time consuming transport tasks, clinical staff are freed to focus on the important task of patient care. This could improve treatment outcomes with increased contact time between medical staff and patients, resulting in faster treatment and reduced waiting times [8]. Additionally, robots also operate consistently without fatigue, enabling 24-hour delivery capability. However, the social concerns must also be considered. The disruption towards portering and logistics staff could potentially lead to mass job losses in roles filled with thousands of workers, raising ethical questions about the impact of robotic automation replacing employment [10]. Furthermore, patients and visitors may feel discontent sharing facilities with autonomous machines, particularly in already high-pressure clinical environments. To address this, the prototype developed for this project included a manual override via the wireless transmitter, ensuring a human operator can always take control of the vehicle.
When identifying legal aspects, it should be noted that autonomous vehicles operating in public or semi-public spaces must comply with strict safety regulations. In the UK, the Automated Vehicles Act 2024 [11] establishes the legal framework for self-driving vehicles, including the necessary requirements for liability and safety standards. Inside hospital premises, autonomous robots must comply with the Supply of Machinery (Safety) Regulations 2008 [12], which retained the safety requirements of the EU Machinery Directive post-Brexit and mandates risk assessments for any mechanical system operating in a workplace. Additionally, the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 [13] places a duty on employers to ensure our equipment does not pose a risk to staff or the public. For this prototype, collision avoidance is managed via the ultrasonic sensor [4]. This was implemented specifically to address safety compliance, ensuring the vehicle halts when an obstacle is detected within a defined distance.
Environmentally, Electric vehicles produce zero direct emissions during operation, making them suitable for indoor hospital use where air quality is critical for patient welfare. The prototype's DC motors [6] and micro:bit microcontrollers [1] consume low amounts of power, and could be powered by rechargeable batteries, minimising waste and reducing long-term energy consumption. However, a full environmental evaluation must also consider the complete lifecycle impact of the electronic components used, the mining of lithium and rare earth materials for batteries and circuit boards carries a significant environmental footprint [14]. The production of the necessary chassis and other components will undoubtably be powered via fossil fuel methods. Furthermore, end-of-life disposal of electronic waste must be managed in accordance with the Waste Electrical and Electronic Equipment (WEEE) Regulations 2013 [15]. For the final deployment, these factors would need to be critically evaluated against the environmental impact of the manual alternatives, such as fuel-powered internal transport vehicles still used in some larger hospital campuses.
