2.3 SWOT Analysis
 
Figure 2: SWOT Analysis
The SWOT analysis identified several weaknesses and threats that were addressed through specific actions during the project.
The most significant weakness was the group's lack of experience with PWM servo control and current management. This became apparent when the FS90 servo included with the Klaw Kit failed during integration. Investigation using the Kitronik documentation and the FS90 datasheet revealed that the servo had been held in a stalled position, drawing current in excess of its rated capacity, which caused permanent damage. To prevent recurrence, the control code was modified to drive the servo in short, timed bursts rather than holding it continuously under load. The team member responsible for diagnosing the fault shared the findings with the rest of the group through a pair programming session, ensuring the knowledge of PWM duty cycles and stall current was not confined to a single member.
A further weakness was limited 3D CAD experience, which resulted in the first print of the payload cage failing to meet the required tolerances. This caused slippage in the Gantt chart as the part had to be redesigned and reprinted. To mitigate the delay, the group reprioritised the remaining tasks so that software development, including radio communication and XOR encryption, proceeded in parallel with the reprinting rather than waiting for the physical part. For future projects, the group identified that buffer time should be allocated for 3D printing and that a smaller test print should be produced first to verify fit before committing to a full-scale part.
The primary external threat was the risk of the build phase overrunning into the time allocated for report writing. This was identified during a weekly progress meeting, and the workload was redistributed so that the group member furthest ahead on the build transitioned to report writing while the remaining members completed assembly and testing. This prevented the report from being rushed at the end of the project.
Throughout the project, the group adopted a consistent approach to addressing skill gaps: identify the deficiency, study the relevant theory and documentation rather than working around the problem, and share the acquired knowledge with the rest of the team through the shared GitHub repository, pair programming, and weekly meetings. By the end of the project, all members had developed working knowledge of micro:bit radio communication, PWM servo control, ultrasonic sensing, breadboard wiring, XOR encryption, and Git version control, all of which are directly transferable to embedded systems and control modules in later years of the course.

