3.1 EV Design Principles
[Explain the theoretical foundations of your EV design. Reference relevant engineering principles.]
The prototype relies on several core engineering principles that underpin the operation of its motor control, sensing, navigation, and communication subsystems. This section outlines the theoretical foundations relevant to the design decisions made throughout the project.
Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)
The vehicle's four DC motors are controlled using pulse width modulation, a technique in which a digital signal is rapidly switched between high and low states at a fixed frequency. The proportion of time the signal remains high within each cycle is known as the duty cycle, and this determines the average voltage delivered to the motor. The relationship is given by:
V_avg = D × V_supply (1)
where V_avg is the average voltage across the motor, D is the duty cycle (ranging from 0 to 1), and V_supply is the supply voltage provided by the Keysight EDU36311A power supply. For example, at a programmed speed of 50 in the code, the PWM register value is 2000 out of a 12-bit range of 4095, giving a duty cycle of approximately 48.8% [2]. At a supply voltage of 6 V, this would deliver an average of approximately 2.93 V to the motor. Higher duty cycles produce higher average voltages, resulting in faster motor speeds.
I2C Serial Communication
The micro:bit communicates with the PCA9685 PWM driver IC on the Kitronik Robotics Board using the Inter-Integrated Circuit (I2C) protocol. I2C is a two-wire serial bus using a clock line (SCL) and a data line (SDA), allowing a master device (the micro:bit) to address and send commands to one or more peripheral devices on the same bus. The PCA9685 receives 12-bit PWM register values from the micro:bit over I2C, which it converts into the corresponding PWM output signals for each motor channel. This architecture offloads the timing-critical PWM generation from the micro:bit's processor, allowing it to focus on higher-level tasks such as sensor reading, radio communication, and decision logic.
H-Bridge Motor Driver
Bidirectional control of each DC motor is achieved through H-bridge circuits, which use four transistors arranged in a bridge configuration to control the direction of current flow through the motor. By activating different pairs of transistors, the current direction is reversed, allowing both forward and reverse rotation from a single supply [6].
 The Kitronik Compact Robotics Board (5693) contains two dual H-bridge driver ICs, each capable of driving two motors independently, providing individual speed and direction control for all four motors [2]. The H-bridge drivers also act as a power stage, allowing the low-voltage 3.3 V control signals from the micro:bit to switch the higher battery voltage to the motors.
Differential Steering
The vehicle does not have a mechanical steering mechanism. Instead, turning is achieved through differential steering, where the left and right motor pairs are driven at different speeds. To turn left, the right-side motors run at full speed while the left-side motors run at a reduced speed, and vice versa for a right turn [6].
 In the line-following mode, the arc correction uses a ratio of 1:4, with the inner wheels driven at one quarter of the outer wheel speed (speed // 4 in the code). This ratio was chosen to provide a gentle correction that keeps the vehicle moving forward while adjusting its heading, rather than a sharp pivot which would cause oscillation and unstable tracking.
Ultrasonic Distance Measurement
The RCWL-1601 ultrasonic sensor measures distance using the time-of-flight principle. The sensor emits a short burst of ultrasonic pulses at 40 kHz and measures the time taken for the echo to return after reflecting off an obstacle. The distance to the obstacle is calculated using:
d = (t × v) / 2 (2)
where d is the distance in metres, t is the round-trip time in seconds, and v is the speed of sound in air, approximately 343 m/s at 20°C [4]. 
The division by 2 accounts for the fact that the sound pulse travels to the obstacle and back. In the MicroPython implementation, the distance is calculated as d = (duration / 2.0) / 29.1, where duration is the echo pulse width in microseconds and 29.1 is the number of microseconds per centimetre at the speed of sound. A source of error in this method is that the speed of sound varies with air temperature, being approximately 331 m/s at 0°C and 349 m/s at 30°C. At a fixed divisor of 29.1, the calculated distance will be slightly overestimated in cold environments and underestimated in warm ones.
Infrared Reflectance Sensing
The line-following sensor operates on the principle of infrared reflectance. Each sensor pair consists of an infrared LED that emits IR light towards the surface and a phototransistor that measures the intensity of the reflected light. Dark surfaces absorb a greater proportion of the incident IR radiation, resulting in less light reaching the phototransistor and a higher output voltage. Light surfaces reflect more IR radiation, producing a lower output voltage [3]. According to the datasheet, at 3 V VCC a typical light surface produces approximately 0.5 V and a dark surface approximately 2.5 V [3]. The sensor output is continuous and analogue, but in this implementation, it is read digitally using a threshold set by the micro:bit's internal comparator at approximately 1.6 V, converting the analogue signal into a binary line/no-line detection.
XOR Encryption
The radio communication between the transmitter and receiver is secured using a bitwise exclusive-or (XOR) cipher. XOR is a logical operation that outputs 1 when the two input bits differ and 0 when they are the same. The key property exploited for encryption is that XOR is its own inverse:
plaintext XOR key = ciphertext (3)
ciphertext XOR key = plaintext (4)
Applying the same key twice recovers the original data, making XOR a symmetric cipher where the same key is used for both encryption and decryption. Each character of the message is XOR-ed with the corresponding character of the shared key, cycling through the key for messages longer than the key length. While XOR with a short repeating key is not considered cryptographically secure for production use, as the repeating key pattern can be exploited through frequency analysis if sufficient ciphertext is captured, it provides adequate protection for a prototype demonstrating the principle of authenticated radio control.

3.2 Key Technologies Used
[Describe the Microbit, Robotics board, sensors, communication modules, and other hardware/software. Include relevant specifications from datasheets.]
The prototype relies on two BBC micro:bit V2 microcontrollers as the core processing units. Each micro:bit is built around a Nordic nRF52833 system-on-chip featuring a 64 MHz ARM Cortex-M4 processor with 512 KB of flash memory and 128 KB of RAM [1]. The V2 revision represents a significant upgrade over the original micro:bit, with four times the processing power and eight times the RAM of the V1 board. The nRF52833 includes an integrated 2.4 GHz radio transceiver, which supports both Bluetooth Low Energy and a proprietary micro:bit radio protocol used for device-to-device communication [1]. This built-in radio capability eliminates the need for external wireless modules, simplifying the communication architecture between the vehicle and the handheld transmitter. 
All software was developed using MicroPython via the Mu editor, chosen for its suitability for rapid prototyping and its direct support for the micro:bit hardware abstraction layer.
Motor control is managed through the Kitronik Compact Robotics Board (5693) [2], which interfaces with the micro:bit via the I2C protocol through a 16-channel PWM driver IC. The board incorporates two dual H-bridge motor driver ICs, enabling independent control of up to four DC motors, along with eight servo outputs [2]. The board accepts a supply voltage range of 3 V to 10.8 V. PWM signals generated by the driver IC allow proportional speed control of each motor by varying the duty cycle, which is essential for executing differential steering during line-following corrections. The vehicle is powered by a Keysight EDU36311A triple-output programmable DC power supply, which provides a regulated and adjustable voltage directly to the Kitronik robotics board during testing. This bench supply was used in place of an onboard battery pack to allow precise control of the supply voltage during characterisation and to eliminate battery discharge as a variable during testing. PWM output signals and motor drive waveforms were verified using a Keysight EDUX1052A digital storage oscilloscope, a 50 MHz, 2-channel instrument with a sample rate of 1 GSa/s.
The four DC motors are mounted within the Waveshare Robot Chassis-P [5], a four-wheeled aluminium alloy platform measuring 200 mm in length and 175 mm in width that provides the structural base for the vehicle.
For autonomous navigation, the Kitronik 5629 line-following board [3] provides two infrared LED and phototransistor sensor pairs, spaced approximately 25 mm apart, which output an analogue voltage proportional to the reflectivity of the surface beneath the vehicle. The board includes onboard tuning potentiometers for adjusting sensor sensitivity and LED indicators for visual confirmation of line detection [3]. These outputs are read by the micro:bit and used to determine whether the vehicle is centred on, drifting left of, or drifting right of the line.
Obstacle detection and collision avoidance are handled by the RCWL-1601 ultrasonic ranging sensor [4]. This sensor operates on a 3 V to 5.5 V supply with a working current of approximately 2.2 mA and provides a detection range of 2 cm to 450 cm, with the most reliable measurements falling within the 10 cm to 250 cm range [4]. The sensor has a resolution of 1 mm and a test cycle of 50 ms. The RCWL-1601 was selected over the more common HC-SR04 specifically because it operates at 3.3 V logic levels natively, making it directly compatible with the micro:bit without requiring a voltage divider on the echo pin [4].
The prototype also incorporates a servo-driven gripper mechanism using the Kitronik Klaw kit (5696), actuated by a 180-degree micro servo (FS90, 9g) operating on a 4.8 V to 6 V supply. This mechanism was integrated to simulate the secure collection and release of payloads at designated delivery points.

