4.2.1 Design
The obstacle detection subsystem uses the RCWL-1601 ultrasonic ranging sensor [4], which measures the distance to objects in front of the vehicle using the time-of-flight principle. The sensor emits a 40 kHz ultrasonic pulse from its transmitter element and measures the time taken for the echo to return after reflecting off a surface. Distance is calculated using the relationship d = (t × v) / 2, where t is the echo pulse duration and v is the speed of sound (approximately 343 m/s at room temperature). The division by two accounts for the fact that the pulse travels to the object and back.
The RCWL-1601 operates on a 3 V to 5.5 V supply with a working current of approximately 2.2 mA, and provides a measuring range of 2 cm to 450 cm [4]. The board dimensions are 40 mm × 18 mm. It was selected over the more common HC-SR04 specifically because it operates at 3.3 V logic levels natively, making it directly compatible with the micro:bit without requiring a voltage divider on the echo pin. The HC-SR04 operates at 5 V logic and would require additional circuitry to avoid damaging the micro:bit's GPIO pins, which are rated for 3.3 V. Eliminating this voltage divider reduced circuit complexity and removed a potential source of measurement error.
The sensor is connected to the micro:bit with the trigger pin on pin0 and the echo pin on pin1. The trigger pin sends a 10 µs HIGH pulse to initiate a measurement, and the echo pin returns a pulse whose width is proportional to the measured distance. The measurement function in the code includes a 30 ms timeout to prevent the program from hanging if no echo is received, for example when the sensor is pointed at open space with no reflecting surface. In this case the function returns -1, which the main control loop treats as a valid reading at safe distance rather than an error, allowing the vehicle to continue operating.
The sensor readings are used to implement a three-zone traffic light system that controls both the vehicle speed and a set of three LEDs (red, yellow, green) connected to pins 15, 14, and 13 respectively. The thresholds are defined as follows:
	Green zone (distance > 30 cm): no obstacle detected. The green LED is illuminated, and the vehicle operates at full SAFE_SPEED. This zone represents normal unobstructed operation.

	Yellow/amber zone (20 cm < distance ≤ 30 cm): obstacle approaching. The yellow LED is illuminated, and the vehicle reduces to CAUTION_SPEED, giving the operator or autonomous system time to react before a potential collision.

	Red zone (distance ≤ 20 cm): obstacle too close. The red LED is illuminated and all motors are stopped. The vehicle will not move forward until the obstacle is cleared and the distance exceeds the caution threshold.
This graduated response avoids sudden stops from full speed, which could destabilise the vehicle or damage the payload. In a hospital environment, abrupt stopping could cause medication or samples to shift or spill inside the payload compartment, so a controlled deceleration through the caution zone is a deliberate safety measure.

