4.2.2 Method
Two categories of tests were conducted on the ultrasonic sensor: a distance accuracy test to compare measured values against known distances, and an oscilloscope analysis of the trigger and echo pulse waveforms under each of the three operating zones.
For the distance accuracy test, an object was placed at nine known distances from the sensor, measured using a metre rule: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40, and 45 cm. At each distance, three ultrasonic readings were taken and recorded via the micro:bit serial output. The mean and sample standard deviation (n-1) were calculated for each distance. This range was chosen because it spans the full operating envelope of the traffic light system, from well within the red zone (5 cm) through the amber zone (20–30 cm) and into the green zone (30+ cm). Distances beyond 45 cm were not tested because the system treats all readings above 30 cm identically (green zone, full speed), so additional accuracy data at longer ranges does not affect the vehicle's behaviour.
For the oscilloscope analysis, a Keysight oscilloscope was used to capture the trigger and echo pulse waveforms under three conditions: red (object close, vehicle stopped), amber (object at mid-range, reduced speed), and green (object far, full speed). The trigger pulse was measured to verify consistent signal generation regardless of distance, and the echo pulse was measured to confirm that pulse width varied proportionally with distance as expected from the time-of-flight relationship. The oscilloscope data was exported as CSV files for analysis.


4.2.3 Optimisation
The primary optimisation for the ultrasonic subsystem was the selection of the distance thresholds. The initial threshold values were set conservatively at SAFE_DIST = 30 cm and CAUTION_DIST = 20 cm. These values were determined through iterative testing during development. Setting the caution threshold too low (for example 10 cm) did not give the vehicle sufficient stopping distance at higher speeds, as the sensor's 50 ms test cycle combined with the motor response time meant the vehicle could travel several centimetres between a reading and the motors actually decelerating. Setting it too high (for example 40 cm) caused the vehicle to slow down unnecessarily in response to objects that were not in its path, particularly in narrow lab environments where walls and furniture were within range.
The 30 cm / 20 cm thresholds were found to provide reliable stopping performance at all tested speeds while avoiding false triggers from objects outside the vehicle's path. The 10 cm gap between the caution and stop thresholds gives approximately 200 ms of warning at typical operating speeds, which is sufficient for the motors to decelerate from CAUTION_SPEED to a full stop.
The timeout value in the measurement function was set to 30 ms (30,000 µs). This corresponds to a maximum measurable distance of approximately 515 cm (using d = (30,000 × 10⁻⁶ × 343) / 2), which exceeds the sensor's rated maximum of 450 cm. A shorter timeout would improve the loop cycle time but could cause valid readings at longer distances to be missed. Since the system only distinguishes between three zones (the furthest boundary being 30 cm), the timeout value is not performance-critical and was left at a conservative value to ensure reliability.
Electrical noise from the H-bridge motor drivers was observed on the echo waveform during oscilloscope testing. This manifested as small voltage fluctuations and ripple on the echo signal, likely caused by the rapid switching of the motor drivers sharing the same supply rail as the sensor. Despite this noise, the pulse edges remained clearly distinguishable, and no false readings were attributed to electrical interference during testing. A dedicated decoupling capacitor on the sensor supply line would reduce this noise further but was not implemented due to time constraints.
